Booting this kernel on a real Raspberry Pi 3 Model B
=====================================================

A step-by-step procedure, with the reason behind each step and an
honest label on what has actually been checked.

EVIDENCE.  doc/raspi3b-completion-plan.ms labels results by what they
were tested against, strongest first:

    hardware        a real Raspberry Pi 3 Model B
    qemu            a device model QEMU already shipped
    qemu+patch      a device model in tools/qemu-patches

Nothing in this file is "hardware".  No real board has been near this
kernel; the completion plan says so in those words.  Every step below
is marked with the strongest evidence it has, and the ones marked
"untested" are exactly where a first bring-up will spend its time.


How the board starts, and why the kernel is a flat binary
---------------------------------------------------------
On a Pi 3 the ARM cores do not fetch the kernel.  The VideoCore GPU
boots first, reads the firmware and config from the first (FAT) SD
partition, loads the ARM kernel image into RAM at 0x80000, and releases
core 0 into it.  There is no ELF loader on the board: the kernel must be
a raw flat binary, and its startup must cope with the exception level
the firmware drops it at.


Step 1 -- build the kernel and the flat image          [qemu+patch]
-------------------------------------------------------------------
    export PATH=$ROOT/MacOSX/arm64/bin:$PATH   # or Linux/<arch>/bin
    cd os/inferno/raspi3b
    mk INIT=raspi3binit install     # the ELF kernel, iraspi3b
    mk kernel8.img                  # the flat image for the firmware

    ("mk boot" does both and reminds you what else the card needs.)

SYSTARG and OBJTYPE need not be given: this board's mkfile sets
OBJTYPE=aarch64 and SYSTARG=$OSTARG, and both mkhost-MacOSX and
mkhost-Linux set OSTARG=os and SHELLTYPE=sh.  Passing them anyway is
harmless here and is a habit worth not forming: pc64's mkfile sets
SYSTARG=Inferno for itself, and carrying "SYSTARG=os" into that
directory asks for a mkfiles/mkfile-os-amd64 that does not exist, which
is how that board got a reputation for not building.

kernel8.img is iraspi3b reduced to its loadable segments laid out at
their link addresses, produced by mkrawimg.py from the ELF program
headers.  7l -H6 emits a headerless image but packs the segments
contiguously, which puts .data at the wrong address; mkrawimg.py honours
the vaddr gap, so a flat load at 0x80000 lands .text at 0x80000 and
.data at 0x100000, matching the link.  Expect:

    wrote kernel8.img: 1601800 bytes, 2 PT_LOAD segments, base 0x80000

and a first instruction of MRS x0, CurrentEL (od -t x1 -N 4 gives
a0 00 38 d5), which is the exception-level test in step 6.


Step 2 -- prove the flat image before touching a card  [qemu+patch]
-------------------------------------------------------------------
QEMU's -kernel wants a Linux Image header for a raw binary, so to boot
the exact bytes the firmware will load, place them with an explicit
loader and start core 0 there:

    dd if=/dev/zero of=sd.img bs=1m count=16
    $QEMU -M raspi3b -m 1G -nographic \
      -drive file=sd.img,if=sd,format=raw \
      -device loader,file=kernel8.img,addr=0x80000,force-raw=on \
      -device loader,addr=0x80000,cpu-num=0

$QEMU must be the patched build (tools/qemu-patches/README).  On a stock
QEMU this boot still reaches a shell, but seven checks report -NONE --
PWM, ARMTIMER, I2S, AUXSPI, SMI, BSCSL and UART-PUTC -- because those
blocks are the ones the patches model.  That is a QEMU you have not
patched, not a kernel fault; the messages name the missing block and its
address ("PWM-NONE no PWM block at 0x3f20c000").

On the patched build this produces a marker set identical to the
ordinary "-kernel iraspi3b" boot the suite uses -- 37 markers, the same
37 -- which is the point of the step: it is the flat image, and the
thing that makes it, that get proven here, not the ELF.

-M raspi3b enters at EL3; real firmware enters at EL2.  So this step
does not exercise the EL2 path.  See step 6.


Step 3 -- partition the card                           [see below]
-------------------------------------------------------------------
READ THIS BEFORE THE FIRST BOOT.  raspi3binit writes to the raw card,
and where it writes was chosen for a blank QEMU image with no partition
table -- tools/boardsuite makes exactly that, dd of zeroes, no MBR.  On
a card partitioned the ordinary way those writes land inside the boot
partition and destroy it.

Every raw-card write the init makes, by sector:

    3               sdcheck single-block write/readback  (SDHOST, sdM0)
    5               sdhcicheck single-block              (Arasan, sdH0)
    8-11, 12-15     the two multi-block write/readbacks
    256-8447        sdfs: "part fs 256 8448", and kfs -r reams all of it

Sector 0 is read and never written, so the MBR survives.  The FAT does
not: a Pi card conventionally starts its first partition at sector 2048,
and 2048-8447 is inside it.  The board would boot once, scribble the
FAT's boot sector, its FATs and its root directory, and never boot again
until the card was rewritten.

So leave sectors 0-8447 unallocated and start the FAT partition at 16384
(8MB) or later.  The firmware reads the MBR and takes the first FAT
partition it finds, wherever it begins, so this costs 8MB and nothing
else.

macOS -- diskutil will not let you name a start sector, but it will
leave a gap, which is the same thing:            [tested on an image]

    diskutil partitionDisk /dev/diskN MBR free FREE 16M MS-DOS BOOT R
    fdisk /dev/rdiskN        # check partition 1's start is past 8447

Run against a 64MB image file this put the FAT partition at sector
31311, type 0x0B.  Check it anyway: the number is diskutil's choice, not
a promise.

Linux:                                                    [untested]

    sudo sfdisk /dev/sdX <<'EOT'
    label: dos
    start=16384, size=524288, type=c, bootable
    EOT
    sudo mkfs.vfat -F 32 -n BOOT /dev/sdX1

No second partition is needed.  The kernel carries its own root
(devroot, built from the root list in the raspi3b configuration), so the
FAT partition holds the firmware and kernel8.img and nothing else.

If you would rather nothing wrote to the card on a bring-up boot, the
three callers are sdhcicheck(), sdcheck() and sdfs() at the top of
os/inferno/init/raspi3binit.b's init().  Dropping sdfs() alone removes
every write above sector 15.


Step 4 -- put the files on the FAT partition           [untested]
-------------------------------------------------------------------
From raspberrypi/firmware, the boot/ directory:

    bootcode.bin
    start.elf
    fixup.dat
    bcm2710-rpi-3-b.dtb
    overlays/disable-bt.dtbo        (keep it in an overlays/ directory)

    git clone --depth 1 https://github.com/raspberrypi/firmware
    cp firmware/boot/{bootcode.bin,start.elf,fixup.dat} /Volumes/BOOT/
    cp firmware/boot/bcm2710-rpi-3-b.dtb               /Volumes/BOOT/
    mkdir -p /Volumes/BOOT/overlays
    cp firmware/boot/overlays/disable-bt.dtbo          /Volumes/BOOT/overlays/

and from here:

    cp config.txt kernel8.img /Volumes/BOOT/

The base .dtb and the overlays directory are not optional even though
this kernel reads no device tree on this board.  dtoverlay= is applied
by start.elf to the board's own .dtb, and a firmware that finds no .dtb
matching the board revision boots in the old ATAG mode and applies no
overlays at all -- silently.  Leave those two files out and everything
still boots, with the PL011 still wired to Bluetooth and the serial
console dead, which looks exactly like a kernel that hung before its
first print.


Step 5 -- wire the console                             [untested]
-------------------------------------------------------------------
config.txt sets arm_64bit=1 and kernel=kernel8.img, and routes the PL011
UART -- which this kernel's console drives, at 0x3f201000 -- to the GPIO
header.  On a Pi 3 the PL011 is wired to Bluetooth by default, so
dtoverlay=disable-bt hands it back to GPIO 14/15.  core_freq=250 pins the
core clock so the baud divisor does not move under you.

    header pin 6    GND
    header pin 8    GPIO14, the Pi's TXD  -> adapter RX
    header pin 10   GPIO15, the Pi's RXD  <- adapter TX

115200 8N1, and a 3.3V adapter -- a 5V one can damage the SoC.  Cross TX
and RX; leave the adapter's 5V pin unconnected and power the Pi from its
own USB supply.

    screen /dev/tty.usbserial-XXXX 115200      (macOS)
    picocom -b 115200 /dev/ttyUSB0             (Linux)


Step 6 -- boot, and what you should see
-------------------------------------------------------------------
The firmware enters a 64-bit kernel at EL2, not EL3.  l.s's core-0 path
reads CurrentEL and drops to EL1 from whichever of EL3 (what QEMU -M
raspi3b gives) or EL2 (real hardware) it finds; an EL3-only drop would
fault on the SCR_EL3 write at EL2.  The EL2 branch has been read, never
run -- this is the first thing that happens on the board and the first
thing to suspect if nothing prints.

Below is a real boot, but an emulated one -- the patched QEMU, a blank
16MB card, no USB devices.  Hardware will differ where noted.

    Inferno/arm64 raspi3b
    ...
    MBOX firmware-rev 346337 arm-mem base 0x0 size 0x3c000000 (MBOX-OK)
    rng: bcm2835 hwrng at 0x3f104000 up (...) RNG-OK
    WDOG-PRESENT loaded and holding while disarmed
    PWM-OK clock, mark/space, balanced, FIFO full/empty and errors
    SYSTMR-OK counter runs, and agrees with the ARM generic timer
    therm: die 25.178 C (adc 719) at 0x3f212000
    board: revision 0x00a02082 serial 0000000000000000
    OTP-OK board identity and customer OTP read (never written)
    DMA-OK control block fetched on a channel the firmware gave us
    AUX-OK mini uart enabled and sent 15 bytes
    ARMTIMER-OK countdown wraps at its programmed rate
    I2S-OK 48kHz frames, both FIFOs, errors, and the frame rate
    AUXSPI-OK gate, FIFO levels, and an 8-bit transaction
    SMI-OK timing registers, FIFO depth, both error paths
    BSCSL-OK addressed by the Pi's own master, both directions
    Inferno Fourth Edition (20151010)
    usbdwc: 8 chan, fifo 4096 words, layout 774+256+512
    ARMTIMER-INTR-OK interrupt arrives at the programmed rate
    UART-PUTC-OK polled writes waited for FIFO room 407 times
    Initial Dis: "/osinit.dis"
    raspi3b init
    RASPI3B-OK
    RANDOM-OK bc6d06e3cc914ded53bf9237c05f7381
    SDHCI-4BIT-OK ... SDHCI-OK
    SD block3 wrote 7071 readback 7071 (SD-WRITE-OK)
    SD-CAP-OK ... SD-OK
    DRAW-OK 640x480
    usbd: 1 root hub(s)
    USB-OK
    SD-FS-CREATE-OK
    RASPI3B-SH-OK

What will read differently on a board:

  - firmware-rev, board revision, serial, die temperature and card
    capacity are all real values there and fixed ones here.

  - USB.  A Pi 3B has an SMSC LAN9514 hub-plus-ethernet soldered to it,
    so usbd should find a hub and an ethernet where this log finds an
    empty root hub, DISK-NONE and ETHER-NONE.  The suite's raspi3b-usb
    configuration is the closer picture (patch 0003 models that part for
    exactly this reason), minus its injected keyboard and mouse.  The
    DWC2 needs its USB power domain enabled through the mailbox
    (SET_POWER_STATE) before it responds; that call is made, and its
    failure ignored, in raspi3b/usbdwc.c -- look there first if USB is
    dead.

  - SDHCI.  Under QEMU the card answers on both controllers, because
    the driver routes it between them through the GPIO alt functions
    (the same mechanism Linux uses) and patch 0002 models that routing.
    On a real Pi 3 the card normally lives on SDHOST and the Arasan
    drives the SDIO wireless part, which rejects SD memory commands.
    If the card half misbehaves, sdhcicheck() is the first suspect and
    dropping that one call is a safe simplification -- sdcheck() and
    sdfs() do not depend on it.


Troubleshooting
-------------------------------------------------------------------
Nothing at all on the serial line
    Almost always the console, not the kernel.  Check: the .dtb and
    overlays/disable-bt.dtbo really are on the card (their absence is
    silent -- see step 4); enable_uart=1 and dtoverlay=disable-bt are in
    config.txt; TX and RX are crossed; ground is connected; 115200 8N1.
    Only after that suspect the EL2 entry path or the MMU/cache gap
    below.

The green ACT LED blinks a pattern and nothing boots
    The firmware never found a kernel.  kernel8.img is missing from the
    card, or the FAT partition is not first, or arm_64bit=1 is absent.

It booted once and never again
    Step 3.  The FAT partition started before sector 8448 and kfs reamed
    it.  Rewrite the card with the gap.

Seven -NONE markers under QEMU
    Stock QEMU rather than the patched build.  Step 2.

Boots, then stops after RASPI3B-OK
    The SD checks are next.  Try a kernel built with sdfs() dropped, and
    then with sdhcicheck() dropped, to separate the card from the
    controller routing.


Known gaps that will bite on hardware and cannot bite here
-------------------------------------------------------------------
These are no-ops under QEMU, which models no caches and drains the SD
FIFO synchronously.  Grep the sources for the notes.

  - MMU enable does not invalidate the caches first (l.s mmuenable).
    The A53's cache reset state is architecturally UNKNOWN and must be
    invalidated before SCTLR.C/I are set.  This is the strongest
    candidate for a board that prints nothing and the reason to look
    here before blaming the console.

  - The SDHOST bio gates each FIFO word on the SDEDM fill level
    (sdhostbcm.c fifospace()/fifodata()), which is real hardware
    behaviour QEMU does not need.  Keep it.

  - Uniprocessor: cores 1-3 are parked at WFE (l.s) and MAXMACH is 1.
    SMP, and the BCM2836 mailbox IPIs, is the next hardware milestone.

  - CMD52/CMD53 (SDIO) have never run: an SD memory card rejects them
    and QEMU models no SDIO device to answer them.
